from pathlib import Path
import re,ast,html
ROOT=Path.cwd()
def section(filename,heading):
 s=(ROOT/'src/content/post'/filename).read_text().split('---',2)[2]
 s=s.split('## '+heading+'\n',1)[1].split('\n## ',1)[0]
 out=[]
 for p in s.strip().split('\n\n'):
  p=re.sub(r'^\*\*[^*]+\*\*\s*','',p)
  if p.startswith(('Evey Huang,','[All research]','[Supporting Workers','[Interacting with','This stream received')):continue
  p=re.sub(r'\[([^\]]+)\]\([^)]+\)',r'\1',p)
  p=html.escape(p).replace('**generation**','<b>generation</b>').replace('**evaluation**','<b>evaluation</b>')
  out.append(('',p))
 return out
old=ast.parse((ROOT/'tmp/pdfs/style-revision/build-before.py').read_text())
REFS={}
for node in ast.walk(old):
 if isinstance(node,ast.Call) and isinstance(node.func,ast.Name) and node.func.id=='page' and len(node.args)>6:
  REFS[ast.literal_eval(node.args[0])]=ast.literal_eval(node.args[6])
INTRO=[
('', 'Teams and organizations accomplish complex work by coordinating expertise, interpreting incomplete information, and evaluating contributions whose relevance depends on what others know and do. AI systems designed around an individual user can assist with idea generation or task completion, but these uses leave open how assistance changes the collaboration through which collective outcomes emerge.'),
('', 'The research presented here examines that relationship across entrepreneurship, science, and knowledge work. We investigate how AI can scaffold the articulation of tacit reasoning, make interaction processes observable at scale, and change the distribution of ideas and judgments across a collective. These questions connect the capabilities of individual systems to the organizational practices through which people use them together.'),
('', 'Our approach combines human-computer interaction, design, and computational social science. Field observation identifies what collaborators need to notice, articulate, and interpret; computational analysis examines patterns across interactions and collections of outputs; system design allows us to study how assistance changes the work in practice. Each method addresses a different source of uncertainty about what support is needed and how its consequences should be evaluated.'),
('', 'Across the projects, AI surfaces possible risks, structures behavioral evidence, or produces contributions that humans must interpret in context. The resulting opportunities depend on the quality of that context, the validity of the measurements, and the authority people retain over how the system guides their work. The portfolio develops these conditions through completed studies, exploratory deployments, and ongoing empirical research.')]
OVERVIEW=[
('Making expertise usable', 'Entrepreneurs seeking assistance must identify what they do not know, locate relevant experience, and explain their needs in a form that another person can address. Our coaching studies examine how mentors scaffold these activities across a learning community, then translate an empirical model of those practices into an AI system. The system prepares novices and mentors differently for their shared conversation, while allowing mentors to revise the knowledge that guides its questions. Related studies of workplace learning and literary judgment examine the capabilities and representations that make tacit knowledge available for use. <b>Pages 3-5.</b>'),
('Understanding collaboration', 'Team processes unfold through sequences of contributions, responses, and adjustments that aggregate outcomes cannot fully explain. We develop a methodological agenda for using language, audio, and visible behavior to study those sequences at greater scale and temporal resolution. Ongoing projects in scientific teams, entrepreneur-investor exchanges, and professional volleyball examine how task demands shape knowledge integration, evaluation, and coordination. These applications require construct-specific validation before behavioral traces can support interpretations of collective work. <b>Pages 6-7.</b>'),
('Evaluating collective outcomes', 'A collection of individually plausible AI outputs may still contain redundant ideas or similar evaluative perspectives. Our work on scientific proposals and reviews examines diversity across generation and evaluation, distinguishing the properties of a collection from the quality of any single contribution. Research on harassment tools adds a question about influence: the people supplying information may have limited authority over how it is classified or acted upon. Together, these projects examine how computational systems distribute knowledge, judgment, and decision authority. <b>Pages 8-9.</b>'),
('', 'The three streams provide complementary foundations for intervention. Coaching research specifies the human practices that can guide AI behavior; multimodal research develops measures of how collaboration unfolds; collective-outcomes research identifies consequences that individual performance measures may miss. Connecting these foundations makes it possible to ask whether assistance grounded in empirical models of collaboration produces better collective outcomes, while preserving differences in context and perspective.'),
('', 'The portfolio closes with research and applied experience, including a system that turns organizational expertise into shared, revisable workflows. This work provides a practical setting in which to examine who specifies common standards, how those standards adapt to unfamiliar tasks, and how users can inspect the reasoning embedded in shared support. <b>Page 10.</b>')]
PAGES=[
(2,'Research overview','RESEARCH OVERVIEW','AI in collective work','Supporting collective work requires understanding how assistance changes what people articulate, how they interact, and which contributions shape subsequent decisions.',OVERVIEW,None,None),
(3,'Coaching expertise','STREAM 01 / CSCW 2023','Making expertise usable','Coaches coordinate learning by translating experience across projects, locating relevant expertise, and helping novices formulate requests that others can address.',section('AI-coaching.md','Understanding coaching expertise')+[
('', 'The contribution is an empirical account of the work required to make a learning community\'s knowledge usable. Accounts of novice challenges primarily came from observations and coaches\' interpretations rather than novice interviews, limiting conclusions about novices\' own experiences. The cognitive model informs the subsequent AI system, while the effectiveness of that intervention requires evidence from its own deployment.')],REFS[3],None),
(4,'AI coaching system','STREAM 01 / CSCW 2025','Scaffolding coaching preparation','The system uses an explicit coaching model to diagnose possible risks and organize context, while mentors interpret that context and revise the model.',section('AI-coaching.md','AI that helps us help each other'),REFS[4],'coaching'),
(5,'Learning and tacit judgment','STREAM 01 / RELATED FOUNDATIONS','Learning and tacit judgment','Making knowledge visible creates a resource for collaboration, but people also need opportunities to develop the capabilities to interpret and use it.',[
('Learning during complex work [3]', 'Complex work requires people to articulate requests, seek and provide assistance, and coordinate expertise as their understanding of a problem changes. Access to colleagues and communication tools creates opportunities for collaboration, but developing the skills to use those opportunities requires support within everyday work. With an interdisciplinary group of collaborators, we developed a workshop proposal and research agenda examining how organizational structures, routines, and technologies can scaffold that learning.'),
('', 'This agenda connects the immediate value of AI-supported preparation to the capabilities people develop through repeated practice. For example, a system may help a novice identify an overlooked risk before one meeting, while the novice\'s ability to recognize similar risks independently remains uncertain. Evaluation should therefore distinguish assistance with the current task from changes in what people learn to notice, articulate, and coordinate over time. The paper proposes a workshop and research agenda; it does not report completed workshop outcomes or an intervention trial.'),
('Representing tacit literary judgments [4]', 'Literary style provides an earlier setting for studying judgments that people can recognize but struggle to describe formally. With Sarah Sterman, Vivian Liu, and Eric Paulos, we conducted formative interviews with 14 participants, elicited crowdsourced comparisons, and developed a neural model of stylistic similarity. Participants\' use of comparisons and examples informed a representation that did not require them to define stylistic categories in advance.'),
('', 'Explorer and Editor interfaces made that representation available for reflection on patterns within and across texts. In an exploratory study with six users, the visualizations prompted participants to articulate distinctions and question assumptions, although model and projection errors could also create confusion. Some users found the overall pattern more useful than exact positions or colors, suggesting that interpretation depended on how the representation was read as well as what it encoded.'),
('', 'The model captured a partial shared signal in judgments with substantial disagreement; it did not establish a universal ground truth for style or improved writing and collaboration. This work informs an approach carried into later projects: elicit tacit reasoning, make selected aspects inspectable, and preserve human interpretation where the computational representation loses context or nuance.')],REFS[5],None),
(6,'Multimodal research agenda','STREAM 02 / JOD PERSPECTIVE','Measuring team interaction','Multimodal AI could make collaboration observable across settings and over time, provided that measurements remain valid for the constructs and contexts being studied.',section('AI-team-science.md','Measuring what matters in teams')+[
('', 'The proposed capability concerns process explanation. A behavioral record could locate when a contribution is taken up, when disagreement escalates, or when a team repairs a breakdown, allowing researchers to examine sequences that an average participation score would obscure. Designing feedback from those records also requires decisions about what teams need to interpret and which inferences the evidence can support.')],REFS[6],None),
(7,'Team interaction studies','STREAM 02 / ONGOING STUDIES','Interaction across settings','Scientific discussion, investor evaluation, and professional sport create different demands for integrating knowledge, responding to challenge, and coordinating interdependent action.',[
('Scientific teams', 'Scientific collaboration requires members to evaluate and connect contributions whose relevance may become apparent only through discussion. We examine integration behavior: how members build on, connect, evaluate, and extend one another\'s ideas. Current analyses suggest that effective teamwork is distinguished in part by what the team subsequently does with its members\' contributions. The manuscript in preparation with Max Chalekson, Matt Groh, D. Abrams, and Brian Uzzi examines these relationships as a foundation for later support that scaffolds integration.'),
('Entrepreneur-investor interaction', 'Questions and challenges require entrepreneurs to clarify assumptions, substantiate claims, and respond to concerns that may not appear in a prepared presentation. With Ava Grey, Brian Uzzi, and Matt Groh, we study how the founder-evaluator exchange reveals preparedness, develops rapport, and relates to evaluators\' judgments. A codebook guides annotation of interaction events; human coding provides a basis for examining agreement; and structured records preserve who contributed, when, and how a response connects to an earlier question. Findings about evaluation outcomes remain to be established.'),
('Professional volleyball', 'Professional volleyball requires tightly interdependent action, with limited time to coordinate between periods of play. We use team huddles to examine how cohesion is enacted through observable interaction and how those dynamics relate to subsequent performance. The study examines behavioral relationships; effects of interventions intended to improve teamwork require separate evaluation.'),
('', 'Across these settings, the program draws on more than 1,000 hours of recorded interaction. Their availability does not imply that all have been annotated or that every construct is validated. Comparing settings allows us to examine which patterns depend on task uncertainty, interdependence, or evaluative pressure, establishing boundaries that context-sensitive AI support would need to respect.')],None,'pipeline'),
(8,'Double compression','STREAM 03 / WORKING MANUSCRIPT','AI and collective outcomes','Evaluating scientific AI requires examining the range of possibilities and judgments it contributes to a decision process, alongside the quality of individual outputs.',section('double-compression.md','Double compression in scientific ideas and reviews')+[
('', 'This distinction matters because idea generation and evaluation allocate attention in different ways: one determines which possibilities enter consideration, while the other shapes the reasons for pursuing or rejecting them. The analysis treats their relationship as a question for further study, rather than demonstrating how the two compressions interact in an operational funding process.')],None,None),
(9,'Participation and influence','STREAM 03 / DIS 2024','Participation and influence','Computational interventions distribute authority through decisions about whose experiences matter, which categories apply, and what responses a system makes available.',section('double-compression.md','Whose judgments shape the response?')+[
('', 'The review contributes an account of how human-centered objectives can become disconnected from implementation and evaluation. Its implications concern the relation between participation and consequential decisions: collecting more information cannot resolve that disconnection unless the system specifies who can interpret the information and how it can change the response. The paper received an Honorable Mention at DIS 2024.')],REFS[9],None),
(10,'Experience','RESEARCH AND APPLIED EXPERIENCE','Research, design, and practice','Field partnerships and applied system development provide settings for examining how organizational knowledge becomes visible, usable, and open to revision.',[
('Research at Northwestern', 'I am a Research Assistant Professor at Northwestern University\'s Kellogg School of Management (July 2026-present), following postdoctoral work with Brian Uzzi and Matthew Groh (February 2025-June 2026). My research connects multimodal studies of team interaction with computational experiments on AI in scientific knowledge production. I independently wrote the proposal for Building Behavioral Process Models from Real Teams with Multimodal AI, which received $75,000 from Microsoft\'s AI and the New Future of Work program, with Brian Uzzi as PI. The related TeamLens proposal was a finalist in the Stanford HAI and Google DeepMind AI for Organizations Grand Challenge.'),
('Shared AI workflows at Novohaven', 'Since October 2025, I have worked as an AI Advisor and Forward Deployment Engineer, designing and developing a platform that makes company practices available through shared workflows. In an international e-commerce business, the structure and quality of AI-assisted deliverables depended on how employees articulated requests and supplied company context. The platform specifies task steps, information requirements, and company standards, with execution histories that allow users to inspect where revision is needed.'),
('', 'Domain experts can describe and revise workflows through natural-language instructions or direct specification of individual steps. The system is designed to surface recurring revision requests, while proposed changes require human review before becoming shared practice. This arrangement assigns the system a role in identifying repeated problems and retains human responsibility for changes to common standards. Effects on learning, quality, and coordination require dedicated empirical evaluation.'),
('Education and other applied work', 'My PhD in Technology and Social Behavior at Northwestern (2024) bridged computer science and communication studies. A sustained partnership with an entrepreneurship incubator connected field observation, cognitive modeling, and system deployment; I also coached engineering students in human-centered design. I earned an MS in the same program (2023) and a BA in Economics from UC Berkeley (2017).'),
('', 'As an AI Technical Advisor to Spark AI (December 2024-December 2025), I advised product and engineering teams on AI features, integration, and deployment for K-12 learning products. At Northwestern Memorial Hospital (summer 2022), I studied older adults\' medication and scheduling workflows in electronic health records and translated usability findings into design recommendations.'),
('', 'Outside work, I play guitar and tennis, take film and digital photographs, read, doodle, and cook spicy food. To discuss related research: <link href="mailto:jiaxinh.evey@gmail.com">jiaxinh.evey@gmail.com</link>.')],None,None)
]
